  <refnamediv>
    <refname>&dhpackage;</refname>

    <refpurpose>prepare audio prompts for playback by rtpproxy</refpurpose>
  </refnamediv>

  <refsynopsisdiv>
    <cmdsynopsis>
      <command>&dhpackage;</command>

      <arg choice="opt"><option>-l</option> <replaceable>limit</replaceable>
      <arg choice="opt"><option>-L</option></arg></arg>

      <arg choice="plain"><replaceable>infile</replaceable></arg>

      <arg choice="opt"><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable></arg>
    </cmdsynopsis>
  </refsynopsisdiv>

  <refsect1>
    <title>DESCRIPTION</title>

    <para><command>&dhpackage;</command> encodes an audio prompt into every
    codec that
    <citerefentry><refentrytitle>rtpproxy</refentrytitle><manvolnum>8</manvolnum></citerefentry>
    can play it out in, using the <command>P</command> (play) command.</para>

    <para><replaceable>infile</replaceable> has to contain raw signed 16-bit
    little-endian mono samples at 8 kHz, with no header. Such a file can be
    produced, for example, with <command>sox</command>:</para>

    <screen>sox prompt.wav -t raw -r 8000 -c 1 -b 16 -e signed-integer \
    -L prompt.sln</screen>

    <para>For each codec, the encoded prompt is written into a file named
    <replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.<replaceable>pt</replaceable>,
    where <replaceable>pt</replaceable> is the RTP payload type of the
    codec. If <replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable> is not given,
    <replaceable>infile</replaceable> is used instead. The following files
    are produced:</para>

    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.0</filename></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>G.711 u-law.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.3</filename></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>GSM 06.10, if GSM support has been compiled in.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.8</filename></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>G.711 A-law.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.9</filename></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>G.722 at 64 kbit/s, if G.722 support has been compiled
          in.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><filename><replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>.18</filename></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>G.729, if G.729 support has been compiled in.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>

    <para>To play the prompt, give <replaceable>outfile_template</replaceable>
    as the prompt name to the rtpproxy <command>P</command> command. rtpproxy
    tries the payload types listed in the command (or those of the session,
    if <literal>session</literal> is given instead) in order and plays the
    first one for which the file exists. Since rtpproxy changes its working
    directory to <filename>/</filename> when it daemonizes (unless started
    with <option>-D</option>), the prompt name should normally be an
    absolute path.</para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>OPTIONS</title>

    <variablelist>
      <varlistentry>
        <term><option>-l</option> <replaceable>limit</replaceable></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>Encode at most <replaceable>limit</replaceable> samples
          (8000 samples per second) of the input.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>

      <varlistentry>
        <term><option>-L</option></term>

        <listitem>
          <para>Loop mode: repeat the input as many times as needed to
          produce exactly <replaceable>limit</replaceable> samples of
          output. Requires <option>-l</option>.</para>
        </listitem>
      </varlistentry>
    </variablelist>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>EXIT STATUS</title>

    <para><command>&dhpackage;</command> exits 0 on success, and 1 if an
    error occurs.</para>
  </refsect1>

  <refsect1>
    <title>EXAMPLES</title>

    <para>Prepare <filename>ringback.sln</filename> for playback as
    <literal>ringback</literal>, producing <filename>ringback.0</filename>,
    <filename>ringback.8</filename> and so on:</para>

    <screen>makeann ringback.sln ringback</screen>

    <para>Make a 30 seconds long prompt out of a shorter one:</para>

    <screen>makeann -l 240000 -L beep.sln beep30</screen>
  </refsect1>

